… bestowing one scroll each upon the Grand Secretaries, the Nine Chief Ministers, the Lecturers-in-Waiting, and the Collators, a memorial of thanks (second year of the Wanli era [1574]).

Five Legal Judgments (pan):
Issuing Service-Record Certificates for Officials and Subofficial Functionaries;
Monopolizing the Payment of Tax Grain;
Prohibiting Ceremonial Welcoming and Sending Off;
Illicitly Selling Military Equipment;
Counterfeiting Auspicious Omens.

Third Session

Five Policy Questions (ce):
Question: The ruler's mind depends solely on how it is nourished, and the work of nourishing and cultivating it must lie in learning. Among the wise kings of antiquity who devoted themselves to canonical learning, foremost praise goes to Gaozong of Yin; as for what is set forth in the "Charge to Yue" (Yue ming), can its words be pointed out and recounted? In subsequent ages there were heroic sovereigns who, whether lecturing on the Classics in the Stone Canal Pavilion (Shiqu ge), visiting the Imperial University (Biyong) to pay respects to the aged, establishing the Institute for the Advancement of Literature (Hongwen guan), or expounding texts in the Hall of Venerating Governance (Chongzheng dian)—did their learning also grasp the essentials of the "Charge to Yue"? When Our Taizu, Emperor Gao, with sage and divine virtue ascended the throne, in his leisure from the myriad affairs of state he immediately discussed the Classics and Histories with his attending officials; …
